Lake Placid"] " / " [:a {:href "/venues/77"} "Olympic Arena, Lake Placid"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/20062"} "47 from 4 countries"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Format"] [:td {:colspan "2"} "Double round-robin tournament."]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "The Canadians were represented by a team mostly from the " [:a {:href "/affiliations/57"} "Winnipeg Hockey Club"] ", called “The Winnipegs”. But the Canadian Olympic Association was concerned that The Winnipegs did not have enough offensive firepower, and added " [:a {:href "/athletes/95551"} "Wally Monson"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/92092"} "Norm Malloy"] " of the Senior A Selkirk Fisherman, and Junior A star " [:a {:href "/athletes/85330"} "Bert Duncanson"] ". Germany initially announced that they would not be sending a team but quickly reversed their decision."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The USA Olympic team played a warm up game against the NHL’s Boston Bruins in January 1932 after which the Canadian authorities asked the IIHF if this would affect the players’ amateur status. " [:a {:href "/athletes/91891"} "Paul Loicq"] " replied that, much to Canadian dismay, since the game was only considered a training exercise, their status would not be changed."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Because of the world-wide depression and the long travel distance to the United States in 1932, only four teams comprised the 1932 Olympic ice hockey field – Canada, the United States, Germany, and Poland. The four played a double round-robin tournament. For the first time in Olympic history, Canada failed to win a game, tying the United States 2-2 in the final game of the Olympic tournament. The other time the two teams met, Canada won but only by 2-1. There were two rinks used in Lake Placid, one outdoors inside the " [:a {:href "/venues/71"} "speed skating oval"] ", and, for the first time at the Winter Olympics, some ice hockey games were " [:a {:href "/venues/77"} "played indoors"] "."] "\n"] [:button {:id "button738